@c ====================================================================
@node Overview
@chapter Overview

Runes aims to do FreeType's job of loading fonts and serving their
metrics, and fontconfig's job of discovering and caching them, without
calling into foreign libraries.  The aim is similar to Google's
Fontations.  It is developed and tested on SBCL.

@menu
* Systems::                     Systems, packages and licenses.
* Requirements::                What each system depends on.
@end menu

@node Systems
@section Systems

@table @code
@item runes
Package @code{RUNES}, nicknames @code{TRUETYPE} and @code{CL-TRUETYPE};
in @file{src/core/}.  The font manager: font discovery, the @code{font}
class, open font loaders, metrics, and glyph positions along a line.
It draws and rasterizes nothing and depends on no graphics library.
@xref{Font manager}.

@item runes/raster
Package @code{RUNES/RASTER}; in @file{src/raster/}.  Rasterizes
strings and single glyphs to 8-bit alpha maps with cl-vectors and
cl-aa.  @xref{Rasterizer}.

@item runes/raster-antron
The same package and API as @code{runes/raster}, rasterizing with
Antron's CPU rasterizer instead of cl-vectors and cl-aa.  Load one of
the two, not both.

@item runes/xcb
Package @code{RUNES/XCB}, nicknames @code{RUNES-XCB},
@code{XCB-TRUETYPE} and @code{XFT}; in @file{src/xcb/}.  Draws text on
X11 through XCB and RENDER glyph sets, and re-exports the
@code{RUNES} and @code{RUNES/RASTER} API.  @xref{X11 bridge}.

@item runes/wayland
Package @code{RUNES/WAYLAND}; in @file{src/wayland/}.  Draws text into
pixels the client owns, as on Wayland.  @xref{Client-side bridge}.

@item zpb-ttf
Package @code{ZPB-TTF}; sources in @file{otf/src/}.  The zpb-ttf font
loader API, kept so that zpb-ttf programs work unchanged, on top of
@code{runes/otf}.  BSD 2-Clause.  @xref{Font loader}.

@item runes/otf
Package @code{RUNES/OTF}, nickname @code{OTF}; in @file{otf/}.
Bounds-checked readers for about 50 OpenType tables, generated from
schemas, plus WOFF and WOFF2 decoding.  MIT.  @xref{OpenType tables}.

@item runes/otf/charstring
Package @code{RUNES/OTF/CHARSTRING}.  The CFF and CFF2 charstring
interpreter and DICT parser, a port of Fontations'.  MIT.
@xref{CFF charstrings}.

@item decompress
Package @code{DECOMPRESS}; in @file{decompress/}.  Brotli, zlib and raw
deflate decoders with no dependencies.  MIT.  @xref{Decompression}.
@end table

For backward compatibility, @file{cl-truetype.asd} loads
@code{runes} and @code{runes/raster}, and @file{clxcb-truetype.asd}
loads @code{runes/xcb}.

Antron renders text from @code{runes} alone: it takes fonts, metrics
and layout from it and rasterizes the outlines itself, so it does not
need @code{runes/raster} or a bridge.

@node Requirements
@section Requirements

@itemize
@item
SBCL.  @code{runes} and the bridges use portable libraries and may
work elsewhere, untested; @code{runes/otf}, @code{zpb-ttf} and
@code{decompress} use SBCL internals and need SBCL.
@item
ASDF 3 and UIOP.
@item
cacle, for the LFU caches of string metrics and alpha maps, and
bordeaux-threads, for the locks around font loaders and caches.
@item
@code{runes/raster}: cl-vectors, cl-paths-ttf and cl-aa, and
trivial-garbage.  @code{runes/raster-antron}: Antron instead of the
cl-vectors libraries.
@item
@code{runes/xcb}: clxcb, the XCB binding.
@end itemize

With the sb-simd-512 build of SBCL (with AVX-512), parts of
@code{decompress} use AVX-512.  Stock SBCL runs the plain Lisp code;
check for @code{:decompress-avx512} in @code{*features*}.

@c ====================================================================
@node Quick start
@chapter Quick start

Load the X11 bridge and index the installed fonts:

@lisp
(asdf:load-system :runes/xcb)
(runes/xcb:cache-fonts)
@end lisp

Wrap an XCB window in a @code{window} object, wait for its Expose
event, and draw on it:

@lisp
(xcb:with-x-connection (conn)
  (let* ((screen (first (xcb:conn-roots conn)))
         (win-id (xcb:generate-id conn))
         (font   (make-instance 'runes/xcb:font
                                :family "DejaVu Sans"
                                :subfamily "Book"
                                :size 24)))
    (xcb:create-window conn 0 win-id (xcb:root screen) 100 100 400 200 0
                       xcb-const:+window-class-input-output+ 0
                       (logior xcb-const:+cw-back-pixel+
                               xcb-const:+cw-event-mask+)
                       :background-pixel (xcb:black-pixel screen)
                       :event-mask xcb-const:+event-mask-exposure+)
    (xcb:map-window conn win-id)
    (xcb:flush conn)
    (let ((win (make-instance 'runes/xcb:window
                              :connection conn :id win-id :screen screen)))
      ;; ... wait for the window's Expose event, then:
      (runes/xcb:draw-text win font "Hello, Runes" 10 60 :colour #xFFFFFF)
      (xcb:flush conn))))
@end lisp

@file{examples/hello.lisp} is the complete program, with the event
loop:

@lisp
(load "examples/hello.lisp")
(runes-example:hello)
@end lisp

@c ====================================================================
@node Font manager
@chapter The font manager

The @code{runes} system, package @code{RUNES}.  Every symbol in this
chapter is also exported from @code{RUNES/XCB}.

@menu
* Font cache::                  Finding the installed fonts.
* Fonts::                       The @code{font} class.
* Font loaders::                Open font files.
* Targets and DPI::             What metrics are measured against.
* Metrics::                     Ascent, descent, text extents.
* Layout::                      Glyph positions along a line.
* Fitting and centring::        Sizing text to a box.
@end menu

@node Font cache
@section The font cache

@defvar *font-dirs*
The directories searched for fonts.  By default
@file{/usr/share/fonts/}, @file{/usr/local/share/fonts/},
@file{~/.local/share/fonts/} and @file{~/.fonts/}, and on macOS also
@file{/Library/Fonts/} and @file{/System/Library/Fonts/}.
@end defvar

@defvar *font-cache*
A hash table of family name to a hash table of subfamily name to
source: the font's pathname, or @code{(@var{pathname} . @var{index})}
for a font in a collection.  Guarded by a lock.
@end defvar

@defun cache-fonts
Scan @code{*font-dirs*} and their subdirectories for font files and
replace @code{*font-cache*} with what is found.  Every font of a
collection is indexed.  The scan builds a new table and swaps it in
when done, so other threads go on finding fonts meanwhile.  Call it
once per image, and again after installing fonts.

A font without a family or subfamily name is left out, since it cannot
be asked for.  The cache holds one file per family and subfamily.  When
two fonts have the same names, as when a font is installed in two
formats, a plain TrueType or OpenType file is preferred over WOFF, and
WOFF over WOFF2, since web fonts are decoded every time they are
opened.  Between two files of the same kind, the one scanned later
replaces the other: a font installed as both @file{.ttf} and
@file{.otf}, or a collection whose fonts share their names.  Files in a
directory are scanned before its subdirectories, in the order the file
system lists them.
@end defun

@defvar *font-file-types*
The file types @code{cache-fonts} reads: @file{ttf}, @file{otf},
@file{ttc}, @file{otc}, @file{woff} and @file{woff2}.
@end defvar

@defun cache-font-file pathname
Add the font in @var{pathname}, or every font of a collection, to
@code{*font-cache*}.  A file the loader cannot read is skipped.
@end defun

@defun get-font-families
Return the list of cached family names.
@end defun

@defun get-font-subfamilies family
Return the subfamilies of @var{family}, such as @samp{Regular},
@samp{Italic} or @samp{Bold}.
@end defun

@defun check-valid-font-families family subfamily
Signal an error unless @var{family} and @var{subfamily} are in the
cache, calling @code{cache-fonts} first if the cache is empty.
@end defun

@defun get-font-pathname font
Return the pathname of the file @var{font} is read from.
@end defun

@defun font-source font
Return where @var{font} is read from: its pathname, or
@code{(@var{pathname} . @var{index})} for a font in a collection.
@code{nil} if the font is not in the cache.
@end defun

@node Fonts
@section Fonts

@deftp {Class} font family subfamily size underline strikethrough overline antialias background foreground overwrite-gcontext
A face, a size and a text style.  Make one with

@lisp
(make-instance 'font :family "DejaVu Sans" :subfamily "Book" :size 24)
@end lisp

@var{size} is in pixels.  @var{subfamily} may be omitted or
@code{nil}; then Regular, Book, or the first available subfamily is
used.  @var{underline}, @var{strikethrough} and @var{overline} default
to false, @var{antialias} to true.  @var{background},
@var{foreground} and @var{overwrite-gcontext} are kept from
clx-truetype and not used by the bridges, which take the colour as an
argument.
@end deftp

@deffn {Accessor} font-family font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-subfamily font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-size font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-underline font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-strikethrough font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-overline font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-antialias font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-background font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-foreground font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-overwrite-gcontext font
The font's slots.  Setting one flushes the font's caches, so a font can
be reconfigured and reused.
@end deffn

@deffn {Generic Function} font-equal font1 font2
True if the two fonts have the same family, subfamily and size, and
the same value in every style slot.
@end deffn

@deffn {Generic Function} flush-font-caches font
Flush everything cached for @var{font}.  A renderer that caches images
per font adds an @code{:after} method to flush those too, as
@code{runes/raster} does.
@end deffn

@deffn {Accessor} font-string-bboxes font
@deffnx {Accessor} font-string-line-bboxes font
The font's caches of string bounding boxes.
@end deffn

@defun make-font-cache font dpi-cache-size string-cache-size inner-provider
@defunx font-cache-fetch cache dpi string
A two-level cache, per DPI and then per string, of values computed by
@var{inner-provider}.  Used for the bounding box caches.
@end defun

@node Font loaders
@section Font loaders

Each font file is opened once, by the zpb-ttf loader (@pxref{Font
loader}), and the loader is kept open for the life of the image.

@defvar *font-loader-cache*
A hash table of pathname to open font loader.
@end defvar

@defmac with-font-loader (loader font) &body body
Run @var{body} with @var{loader} bound to @var{font}'s cached loader,
opening it the first time, while holding the font loader lock.  A
loader is not thread-safe, so use it only inside this macro.
@end defmac

@defun close-font-loaders
Close every cached loader and empty @code{*font-loader-cache*}.
@end defun

@node Targets and DPI
@section Targets and DPI

The metric functions take a @dfn{target}, which gives the resolution
the text is measured at: an integer DPI, a cons @code{(@var{dpi-x}
. @var{dpi-y})}, or, with a bridge loaded, a drawable or a screen.
Anything else counts as 96 DPI.

@deffn {Generic Function} target-dpi target
Return @code{(values @var{dpi-x} @var{dpi-y})} for @var{target}.  A
backend adds methods for its own drawables and screens.
@end deffn

@defun font-units->pixels-x dpi-x font
@defunx font-units->pixels-y dpi-y font
Return the factor that converts font units to pixels.
@end defun

@node Metrics
@section Metrics

All lengths are in pixels.  The @dfn{bounding box} of a string is its
ink: the box around the glyphs as drawn.  The @dfn{line box} spans the
advance widths horizontally and the font's ascent and descent
vertically, which is what lines laid out one under another need.

@defun font-ascent target font
@defunx font-descent target font
@defunx font-line-gap target font
@defunx baseline-to-baseline target font
The font's ascent, descent (negative, as in TrueType), line gap, and
the distance between the baselines of two lines.
@end defun

@defun font-ascent-for-dpi dpi-y font
@defunx font-descent-for-dpi dpi-y font
The same as @code{font-ascent} and @code{font-descent}, for a DPI.
@end defun

@defun font-lines-height target font lines-count
The height of @var{lines-count} lines: ascent plus descent for one
line, plus the line gap between lines.
@end defun

@defun text-bounding-box target font string &key start end
@defunx text-width target font string &key start end
@defunx text-height target font string &key start end
The bounding box of @var{string}, from @var{start} to @var{end}, and
its width and height.
@end defun

@defun text-line-bounding-box target font string &key start end
@defunx text-line-width target font string &key start end
@defunx text-line-height target font string &key start end
The same for the line box.
@end defun

@defun xmin bounding-box
@defunx ymin bounding-box
@defunx xmax bounding-box
@defunx ymax bounding-box
The sides of a bounding box.
@end defun

@defvar *allow-fixed-pitch-p*
When true, the default, the width of a line in a font marked
fixed-pitch is the first glyph's advance times the length.  Faster,
but wrong for fonts whose CJK glyphs are double width despite the
flag; bind it to @code{nil} for those.
@end defvar

@node Layout
@section Layout

@defun layout-string font string &key (dpi-x 96) (letter-spacing 0.0) (word-spacing 0.0) kerning-table
Lay out @var{string} on one line.  @var{letter-spacing} is added after
each character, @var{word-spacing} after each space.  Kerning comes
from @var{kerning-table} or else from the font.  Return
@code{(values @var{layout} @var{total-advance})}, where @var{layout}
is a vector of @code{(@var{glyph-index} @var{pen-x})}, @var{pen-x} the
exact pen position in pixels from the start of the line.
@end defun

@defun kern-offset font char1 char2 &key custom-table (dpi-x 96)
The kerning between @var{char1} and @var{char2} in fractional pixels,
from @var{custom-table} if given, else from the font.
@end defun

@defun line-box font dpi &key (multiplier 1.0) explicit-height
Return @code{(values @var{ascent} @var{descent} @var{height}
@var{leading})} in pixels, @var{ascent} and @var{descent} both
positive.  @var{height} is the natural line height times
@var{multiplier}, or @var{explicit-height}.
@end defun

@defun font-decorations font string x y dpi-x dpi-y line-p
The underline, strikethrough and overline of @var{string} drawn at
baseline (@var{x}, @var{y}), as a list of rectangles @code{(@var{left}
@var{top} @var{width} @var{height})}, y down.  They span the bounding
box, or the line box when @var{line-p}.  @code{nil} when the font has
none of the three set.
@end defun

@node Fitting and centring
@section Fitting and centring

@defun fit-font target family subfamily string box-width box-height &key (min-size 8) (probe-size 100)
Return a new font of @var{family} and @var{subfamily}, as large as
possible with @var{string}'s line box inside @var{box-width} by
@var{box-height}, and at least @var{min-size}.
@end defun

@defun fit-font-size target family subfamily string box-width box-height &key (min-size 8) (probe-size 100)
Like @code{fit-font}, but return only the size, for a caller that
resizes a font of its own.
@end defun

@defun text-center target font string box-width box-height
Return @code{(values @var{baseline-x} @var{baseline-y})} that centre
@var{string} in a box of @var{box-width} by @var{box-height}, to pass
to @code{draw-text-line}.
@end defun

@c ====================================================================
@node Rasterizer
@chapter The rasterizer

The @code{runes/raster} system, package @code{RUNES/RASTER}, turns
glyph outlines into 8-bit alpha maps (A8: one byte of coverage per
pixel).  @code{runes/raster-antron} has the same package and API and
rasterizes with Antron; load one or the other.

Glyphs are rasterized once per face, size and DPI and kept on the
client.

@defvar *max-glyph-faces*
The most faces, each at one size and DPI, whose glyphs are kept.  Past
it they are dropped and rasterized again when next used.  The default
is 256.
@end defvar

@defvar *glyph-images*
The glyph images kept, per face.
@end defvar

@deftp {Structure} glyph-image
One glyph rasterized for one face: an A8 image with rows padded to 4
bytes, its origin relative to its top left, and its advance.
@end deftp

@defun glyph-image-data image
@defunx glyph-image-width image
@defunx glyph-image-height image
@defunx glyph-image-x image
@defunx glyph-image-y image
@defunx glyph-image-advance image
@defunx glyph-image-stride image
The pixels, size, origin (@var{x}, @var{y}) and advance in pixels of
@var{image}, and its bytes per row (the width rounded up to 4).
@end defun

@defun layout-glyphs font string dpi-x dpi-y
Lay out @var{string} and rasterize the glyphs not yet rasterized for
this face.  Return a vector of @code{(@var{glyph-index} @var{pen-x}
@var{image})} and the face key.
@end defun

@defun layout-styled-glyphs font string &key (dpi-x 96) (dpi-y 96) (letter-spacing 0.0) (word-spacing 0.0) kerning-table
Like @code{layout-glyphs}, with the options of @code{layout-string}.
Return @code{(values @var{layout} @var{total-advance} @var{key})}.
@end defun

@defun text-pixarray target font string
@defunx text-pixarray dpi-x dpi-y font string
@defunx text-line-pixarray target font string
@defunx text-line-pixarray dpi-x dpi-y font string
Rasterize the whole string into one alpha map, across its bounding box
or line box.  Return five values: the alpha map, @var{min-x},
@var{max-y}, the width and the height.  The bridges draw glyph by
glyph instead; these are for code that wants one image of a string.
@end defun

@defun text-pixarray-for-dpi dpi-x dpi-y font string
@defunx text-pixarray-for-drawable drawable font string
@defunx text-line-pixarray-for-dpi dpi-x dpi-y font string
@defunx text-line-pixarray-for-drawable drawable font string
The two calling forms of @code{text-pixarray} and
@code{text-line-pixarray} as separate functions.
@end defun

@c ====================================================================
@node X11 bridge
@chapter The X11 bridge

The @code{runes/xcb} system, package @code{RUNES/XCB}, draws text on
X11 through XCB (the clxcb binding) and the RENDER extension.  Each
glyph is uploaded once per connection into a RENDER glyph set, and a
string is drawn with one small request naming its glyphs, as Xft does.

@menu
* Drawables::                   Windows and pixmaps to draw on.
* Drawing text::                @code{draw-text} and @code{draw-text-line}.
* X11 caches::                  What is kept per connection.
* RENDER helpers::              Lower-level functions.
@end menu

@node Drawables
@section Drawables

@deftp {Class} drawable connection id visual screen owned
The abstract base of @code{window} and @code{pixmap}: an XID text is
drawn on, with its connection, so that the two cannot be mismatched.
The visual defaults to the screen's root visual.
@end deftp

@deftp {Class} window
@deftpx {Class} pixmap
A window or a pixmap that text is drawn on.
@end deftp

Whoever creates an X resource frees it.  A drawable always frees the
RENDER picture it makes for drawing, and frees the window or pixmap
itself only when made with @code{:owned t}:

@lisp
(make-instance 'runes/xcb:window :connection c :id win)
    ;; frees only its picture: the window is the application's
(make-instance 'runes/xcb:pixmap :connection c :id pm :owned t)
    ;; also frees the pixmap
@end lisp

@deffn {Generic Function} destroy-drawable drawable
Free @var{drawable}'s picture, and the window or pixmap too if
@var{drawable} owns it.  Can be called more than once.
@end deffn

@defun release-drawable drawable
Free the picture and cancel the finalizer, but leave the window or
pixmap alone, owned or not; @var{drawable} no longer owns it
afterwards.
@end defun

Every drawable has a finalizer as a safety net.  It does not write to
the X connection, which belongs to the application's thread: it queues
the free requests instead.

@defun flush-deferred-frees connection
Send the free requests queued by finalizers for @var{connection}.  Call
it from the thread that owns the connection.  @code{draw-text},
@code{draw-text-line} and @code{destroy-drawable} call it; a program
that only measures text and abandons drawables should call it itself.
@end defun

@deffn {Accessor} drawable-connection drawable
@deffnx {Accessor} drawable-id drawable
@deffnx {Accessor} drawable-visual drawable
@deffnx {Accessor} drawable-owned-p drawable
@deffnx {Generic Function} drawable-screen drawable
The drawable's connection, XID, visual, ownership and screen.
@end deffn

@deffn {Generic Function} dpi-x drawable
@deffnx {Generic Function} dpi-y drawable
@deffnx {Function} screen-dpi screen
The resolution of a drawable or a screen, from the screen's size in
pixels and millimetres.
@end deffn

@node Drawing text
@section Drawing text

@defun draw-text drawable font string x y &key (colour #xFFFFFF)
@defunx draw-text-line drawable font string x y &key (colour #xFFFFFF)
Draw @var{string} with its baseline at (@var{x}, @var{y}).  The
underline, strikethrough and overline span the bounding box for
@code{draw-text} and the line box for @code{draw-text-line}, which is
what lines laid out one under another want.  @var{colour} is an
@code{#x00RRGGBB} integer.  Antialiasing follows
@code{font-antialias}.

Each glyph's origin is put on a whole pixel, so a line is never more
than half a pixel off its measured width, and decorations are whole
pixels.  Neither function fills a background; draw it first.
@end defun

@defvar *draw-with-glyph-sets*
When true, the default, text is drawn through glyph sets.  When
@code{nil}, the whole string is rasterized and uploaded as one alpha
mask on every call, as clx-truetype did.
@end defvar

@node X11 caches
@section Caches

A connection keeps at most 64 glyph sets, one per face, size, DPI and
antialiasing, and frees the least recently used.  The tables below are
weak on the connection, so none keeps a connection alive, and the
server frees what they hold when the connection closes.

@defvar *fill-cache*
Per connection, a table of colour to solid-fill picture.
@end defvar

@defvar *mask-gc-cache*
Per connection and screen, the depth-8 GC used to upload alpha masks.
@end defvar

@node RENDER helpers
@section RENDER helpers

These are used by the drawing functions and exported for programs
that use RENDER themselves.

@defvr {Constant} +pict-op-over+
RENDER's Over operator.
@end defvr

