@c ====================================================================
@node Client-side bridge
@chapter The client-side bridge

The @code{runes/wayland} system, package @code{RUNES/WAYLAND}, draws
text into pixels the client owns, as Wayland clients do.  It does not
talk to a Wayland compositor: the client passes functions that blend
into its own surface, so it keeps its clipping and fast paths.

@defun draw-text font string x y &key (dpi 96) blend fill
Draw @var{string} with its baseline at (@var{x}, @var{y}).  For each
glyph, @var{blend} is called as @code{(blend @var{left} @var{top}
@var{width} @var{height} @var{data} @var{stride})}, to blend the text
colour through @var{data}, an A8 alpha map with rows @var{stride}
bytes apart.  For each decoration, @var{fill} is called as @code{(fill
@var{left} @var{top} @var{width} @var{height})}.  Decorations span the
bounding box.
@end defun

@defun draw-text-line font string x y &key (dpi 96) blend fill
Like @code{draw-text}, with decorations across the line box.
@end defun

@defun draw-text-into-buffer pixels width height font string x y colour &key (dpi 96) line-p
Draw into @var{pixels}, a vector of @var{width} by @var{height}
@code{#x00RRGGBB} pixels, in @var{colour}.  @var{line-p} spans the
decorations across the line box.
@end defun

@defun blend-alpha-into-buffer pixels width height left top w h data stride colour
@defunx fill-rectangle-in-buffer pixels width height left top w h colour
The blend and fill that @code{draw-text-into-buffer} uses, clipped to
the buffer.
@end defun

@defun glyph-run font string dpi
@var{string} laid out and rasterized: a vector of @code{(@var{pen-x}
. @var{glyph-image})}, @var{pen-x} in whole pixels from the start of
the line.  Runs are cached per font, string and DPI.
@end defun

@defvar *max-glyph-runs*
The most glyph runs kept.  Past it all are dropped.
@end defvar

@defun flush-glyph-runs &optional font
Forget the glyph runs of @var{font}, or of every font.
@code{flush-font-caches} does this for its font.
@end defun

@c ====================================================================
@node Font loader
@chapter The font loader

The @code{zpb-ttf} system, package @code{ZPB-TTF}, keeps the API of
Zachary Beane's zpb-ttf on top of @code{runes/otf}, so existing
programs work unchanged and can open more fonts: TrueType and CFF/CFF2
outlines, variable fonts, collections, WOFF and WOFF2.  The original
zpb-ttf manual is @file{otf/doc/index.html};
@file{otf/doc/font-loader-details.md} lists what differs from the
original and what is not supported.

A font loader is not thread-safe: it fills its glyph cache lazily.

@lisp
(zpb-ttf:with-font-loader (font "LiberationSans-Regular.ttf")
  (let ((glyph (zpb-ttf:find-glyph #\A font)))
    (values (zpb-ttf:family-name font)
            (zpb-ttf:advance-width glyph)
            (zpb-ttf:bounding-box glyph))))
@end lisp

