@c ====================================================================
@node Decompression
@chapter Decompression

The @code{decompress} system, package @code{DECOMPRESS}, decodes
Brotli (RFC 7932), zlib (RFC 1950) and raw deflate (RFC 1951) data held
in memory.  It has no dependencies and works on its own.  It neither
streams nor compresses.  The input is read in place.

@defun decompress input &key size-hint (start 0) end
Decode the Brotli stream in @var{input}, an octet vector, between
@var{start} and @var{end}, and return the decoded octets.
@var{size-hint}, the decoded size if known (WOFF2 records it), lets the
output be allocated once.
@end defun

@defun zlib-decompress input &key size-hint (start 0) end
@defunx inflate input &key size-hint (start 0) end
Decode a zlib stream (header, deflate data, Adler-32), or raw deflate
data, in the same way.
@end defun

@deftp {Condition} decompression-error
@deftpx {Condition} brotli-error
@deftpx {Condition} deflate-error
Malformed input.  @code{brotli-error} and @code{deflate-error} are
subtypes of @code{decompression-error}.
@end deftp

The Brotli decoder needs the Brotli dictionary,
@file{decompress/data/dictionary.bin}, read the first time a stream uses
it.  An image saved with @code{save-lisp-and-die} should load it first.

@file{decompress/README.md} has the measurements against libbrotli,
zlib-ng and libdeflate, and @file{decompress/doc/optimizations.md}
records what was tried.

@c ====================================================================
@node Migrating from clx-truetype
@chapter Migrating from clx-truetype

The X11 bridge started as clx-truetype ported from CLX to XCB.
@file{doc/design-notes.md} explains the changes in detail.

@itemize
@item
XCB, not CLX: no @code{xlib} symbols; everything draws through a clxcb
connection.

@item
@code{window} and @code{pixmap} wrap an XID and its connection, and
free the window or pixmap only when made with @code{:owned t}.
clx-truetype's always freed it, even an application's own window.

@item
The colour is an argument, @code{:colour #x00RRGGBB}, since XCB cannot
read a GC's foreground back.

@item
Text is drawn through RENDER glyph sets: about 30 times faster for a
repeated string, over 100 times for a new one, and a few bytes per
character over @command{ssh -X}.  Glyphs sit on whole pixels, the
decorations are whole-pixel lines, and @code{draw-text-line} no longer
clips the overline.

@item
No background fill: draw the background first.

@item
Caches are weak hash tables keyed on the connection, and finalizers
queue their frees for the application's thread.

@item
@code{window} and @code{pixmap} are exported names: a package that uses
both this one and CLX or CLIM should shadow them.
@end itemize

